text:
your main layer shaded by one numerical variable;
a context layer (boundaries) on the map;
a legend that explains the variable and units;
a pop-up with a value and an interpretive note;
notes on the five questions and two or three limitations of your data.

By the end of the session your story map should have:

a title, your candidate number and group number, and no name;
your main headings in place, even if some are still empty;
your web map embedded, with legend and zoom, and working pop-ups;
at least one photo with a caption;
a link that opens when you are signed out.

You have until the worksheet deadline to finish. Use the remaining time for the text, the remaining photos and videos, and for checking your story map against the brief.

By the end of the session your saved map should have:

a basemap of your choice;
your working group’s sites, and only those;
your route as a line;
clear styling for both layers;
pop-ups that show the site ID, note and photo. 

If you finish early: use the Measure tool (right-hand toolbar) to find how far you walked, try a different basemap, or add labels with the site IDs (Labels on the right-hand toolbar).

A good description of a variable states:

what was measured, in which units, for which units of analysis (LSOAs, people), and how many;
the centre and spread, with the right measures (median and IQR for skewed data);
the shape, with a figure;
how groups differ, in numbers and in a figure, with careful wording.

For example: “Across London’s 4,994 LSOAs, modelled annual mean NO2 in 2022 had a median of … µg/m³ (IQR …). Every LSOA exceeded the WHO guideline of 10 µg/m³, and …% exceeded the interim target of 20 µg/m³.” Write two or three sentences like this for PM2.5.

a project GEOG0187-stats with the data in data/ and a saved script w05-describing.R;
you can use select(), filter(), mutate(), arrange(), group_by() + summarise() and count();
a description (centre, spread, shape) of NO2 and PM2.5 with a histogram;
a comparison of NO2 between groups, with a boxplot;
two or three sentences that describe the data, written as you would in the worksheet.
